From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: mediatek: add the MT6897 AFE and its machine driver

Register layout and bring-up values come from the MediaTek MT6897 driver
in the OnePlus kernel (oneplus/mt6897_b_16.0.0_oneplus_pad), the structure
from the mainline MT8196 driver. Of 1415 register addresses with the same
name in both, 1360 match. The others are the ETDM_OUT blocks and the TDM
block. The interrupts are also cleared differently, by writing
AFE_IRQ_MCU_CLR.

Unlike MT8196, the firmware expects the SiP audio calls on this SoC: the
domain sidebands when the clocks come up and the SRAM and DRAM requests
for the SPM. MediaTek's driver makes all of them.

This starts with what the Lenovo TB375FC needs for its speakers and
microphones: three stereo memifs, four uplink ones, ETDM 4 and the capture
side of ADDA. ETDM_OUT4 masters the link, the clocks leave on the I2SIN4
pads and ETDM_IN4 follows it. Its data is taken from ETDM_OUT4 with the
"I2SIN4 Loopback" control, which allows checking the frames without a codec.
The internal path misses the pads, so that ETDM_IN4 then samples two bit
clocks later: at the vendor's value for the pads, the samples come back
shifted left by two bits.

The machine driver fixes the link to the 48 kHz, four slots of 32 bits
that the amplifiers' register files program. The AFE has no rate
converter in that path, so the front ends take 48 kHz only.

The machine driver also runs the calibration of the MTKAIF link to the
MT6368, as the vendor's does.

The stereo memifs can reach any of the four slots through the
interconnect, so the board's speaker layout is a matter of mixer
settings.
